  "description": "Native-precision DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash on four RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell cards with 125 GiB of host RAM, using Local Inference Lab vLLM jovian-judgement r38 unchanged: TP4, B12X attention/MoE/linear, FP8 KV, DSpark K7 with adaptive verification, vision, 524,288 context, 4096 batched tokens, 16 sequences, breakable CUDA graphs. Engram tables are read from disk with io_uring, so the host does not need the ~189 GiB that RAM mode pins; the container needs unlimited memlock and a seccomp profile that allows io_uring (Docker default plus three syscalls, shipped with the evidence). blackwell-forge (2026-09-26) pins --kv-cache-memory-bytes 11274289152 (10.5 GiB/GPU), which skips the profiling graph pass and uses the utilisation slack: KV pool 7,636,766 -> 8,536,802 tokens (16.28x at 524k), warm boot 205 -> 161 s, coherence ok at 391K; measured C1 146 prose / 244 code, C4 268, C8 453 aggregate tok/s, cold 32K prefill 6.5-6.7K. A cold 500,000-token prefill and eight concurrent cold 150,000-token prompts completed with flat GPU memory. Replaces the SGLang recipe on the same host, which hit CUDA OOM in the low-ratio indexer on cold prefills past about 290k tokens. Host GPUs are capped at 275 W without a P2P driver override, so decode is below Local Inference Lab's published r38 figures.",
